            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Nuruddin Jahangir: Ascension, Zanjir-i-Adl & 12 Royal Decrees</h3>\n            <p>Following the death of Akbar in October 1605, his eldest surviving son, <strong>Prince Salim</strong>, ascended the throne at Agra Fort on November 3, 1605, assuming the grandiose regnal title <strong>Nuruddin Muhammad Jahangir Badshah Ghazi</strong> ('Light of the Faith, World-Seizer').</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Golden Chain of Justice (<em>Zanjir-i-Adl</em>)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Institution of Direct Petitions:</strong> In an effort to demonstrate his personal devotion to justice, Jahangir installed a famous <strong>Golden Chain of Justice (<em>Zanjir-i-Adl</em>)</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Specifications:</strong> Made of pure solid gold, measuring 80 feet in length and weighing several quintals, the chain was strung with <strong>60 golden bells</strong>. It was fastened between the Shah Burj (Royal Tower) of Agra Fort and a stone post erected on the opposite bank of the Yamuna River.</li>\n                <li><strong>Direct Access:</strong> Any aggrieved subject, regardless of social rank, who was denied justice by lower officials could bypass imperial bureaucrats and pull the chain day or night, ringing the bells in the emperor's private quarters to demand an immediate personal imperial audience.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Twelve Royal Decrees (<em>Dastur-ul-Amal</em>)</h4>\n            <p>Immediately upon his accession, Jahangir promulgated twelve historic administrative edicts (<em>Dastur-ul-Amal</em>) aimed at promoting public welfare and securing commercial confidence:</p>\n            <ol>\n                <li><strong>Abolition of Cesses:</strong> Prohibited the collection of unauthorized transit duties and market cesses, such as <em>Tamgha</em> and <em>Mir Bahri</em> (river tolls).</li>\n                <li><strong>Highway Security & Sarais:</strong> Ordered local jagirdars to build sarais and dig wells along robbery-prone highways to protect traveling caravans.</li>\n                <li><strong>Protection of Inheritances:</strong> Decreed that the property of deceased persons should pass unmolested to legal heirs; if an heir was lacking, the estate was used by the state to construct mosques and bridges.</li>\n                <li><strong>Prohibition of Intoxicants:</strong> Banned the manufacture, transport, and sale of wine, opium, and intoxicating liquors throughout the empire (despite his own personal addiction).</li>\n                <li><strong>Ban on Mutilation:</strong> Forbade horrific punishments like the cutting off of noses and ears.</li>\n                <li><strong>Prohibition of Forced Seizures:</strong> Forbade imperial officials from forcibly acquiring peasant houses or private lands.</li>\n                <li><strong>Hospital Construction:</strong> Established state-funded free hospitals (<em>Shifakhanas</em>) staffed by qualified physicians in major imperial cities.</li>\n                <li><strong>Slaughter Regulations:</strong> Banned the slaughter of animals on two days every week—Thursday (his day of accession) and Sunday (Akbar’s birthday).</li>\n                <li><strong>Confirmation of Mansabs:</strong> Confirmed all existing nobles in their ranks and honors.</li>\n                <li><strong>Madad-i-Maash Confirmation:</strong> Re-endorsed charitable religious tax-free land grants (<em>Suyurghal</em> / <em>Madad-i-Maash</em>).</li>\n                <li><strong>General Amnesty:</strong> Released long-term political prisoners across imperial fortresses.</li>\n                <li><strong>Prohibition of Jagir Confiscation:</strong> Safeguarded jagirdars from arbitrary dismissal without imperial inquiry.</li>\n            </ol>\n        ",
