            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Rebellion of Prince Khusrau, Guru Arjan Dev’s Execution & Sikh Estrangement</h3>\n            <p>Within six months of Jahangir's accession, the Mughal Empire was shaken by a bitter dynastic rebellion that ignited a fateful ideological alienation between the Mughal state and the Sikh community.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Rebellion of Prince Khusrau (April 1606 AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Popularity of Khusrau:</strong> Prince Khusrau was Jahangir's eldest son, born to Man Bai (daughter of Raja Bhagwant Das of Amber). Exceptionally handsome, cultured, and of unblemished moral character, Khusrau had been favored by powerful nobles like Raja Man Singh and Mirza Aziz Koka to succeed Akbar.</li>\n                <li><strong>Flight from Agra:</strong> On April 6, 1606, Khusrau fled from Agra Fort toward Punjab with 350 cavalrymen, attracting thousands of disaffected troopers along the route and laying siege to Lahore.</li>\n                <li><strong>Battle of Bhairowal:</strong> Jahangir pursued his son with relentless energy. At the <strong>Battle of Bhairowal</strong> on the Beas River, the imperial army routed Khusrau's makeshift force. Khusrau attempted to cross the Chenab River to reach Kabul, but was captured by imperial officers.</li>\n                <li><strong>Severe Punishment:</strong> Jahangir forced Khusrau to ride on an elephant through a gruesome double avenue of his impaled followers outside Lahore. Khusrau was imprisoned, later partially blinded, and eventually strangled to death at Burhanpur in 1622 by order of his younger brother, Prince Khurram (Shah Jahan).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Execution of the Fifth Sikh Guru, Guru Arjan Dev (May 1606 AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Encounter at Goindval:</strong> During his flight through Punjab, Khusrau visited <strong>Goindval</strong> on the banks of the Beas River to seek the blessings of the fifth Sikh Guru, <strong>Guru Arjan Dev</strong>. The Guru, in accordance with the Sikh spiritual tradition of extending hospitality and comfort to any distressed seeker, blessed the prince and applied a saffron mark (<em>tilak</em>) to his forehead.</li>\n                <li><strong>Imperial Retaliation:</strong> Jahangir, who in his memoirs recorded his long-standing prejudice against the Guru’s immense spiritual influence over both Hindus and Muslims, viewed the blessing as active political treason.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Tragic Martyrdom:</strong> Jahangir summoned Guru Arjan Dev to Lahore and imposed a crushing fine of two lakh rupees, which the Guru refused to pay. Jahangir handed the Guru over to <strong>Murtaza Khan</strong> (Shaikh Farid Bukhari). After enduring days of horrific torture (sitting on red-hot iron plates and boiling sand), Guru Arjan Dev achieved martyrdom on <strong>May 30, 1606</strong> on the banks of the Ravi River.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Historical Ramifications: Transformation of the Sikh Panth</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>The execution of Guru Arjan Dev proved to be an epochal watershed in Indian history. It fundamentally altered the pacifist devotional character of the early Sikh movement.</li>\n                <li>Guru Arjan’s son and successor, the sixth Guru, <strong>Guru Hargobind</strong>, militarized the Sikh community, adopting the dual doctrine of <strong>Miri and Piri</strong> (temporal and spiritual sovereignty), wearing two swords, building the <strong>Akal Takht</strong> (Throne of the Timeless) opposite the Harmandir Sahib, and raising a standing Sikh cavalry army to resist imperial tyranny.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
