            "id": "m11",
            "title": "Aurangzeb’s Religious Retrenchment: Reimposition of Jizya, Temple Demolitions & Fatawa-i-Alamgiri",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Aurangzeb’s Religious Retrenchment: Reimposition of Jizya, Temple Demolitions & Fatawa-i-Alamgiri</h3>\n            <p>Aurangzeb’s domestic policy marked a fundamental ideological reversal of the inclusive, secular foundations laid by Akbar. Believing that imperial decline stemmed from religious laxity, he attempted to convert the multi-cultural Mughal Empire into an orthodox Islamic state governed strictly by the Hanafi school of Sharia.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Reimposition of the Jizya Poll Tax (April 1679 AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Imperial Firman:</strong> In April 1679, after a lapse of 115 years (having been abolished by Akbar in 1564), Aurangzeb formally reimposed the discriminatory <strong>Jizya tax</strong> upon all non-Muslim subjects (<em>dhimmis</em>) throughout his empire.</li>\n                <li><strong>Public Opposition:</strong> Thousands of Hindu citizens gathered outside the Red Fort in Delhi to peacefully petition against the tax. Aurangzeb ordered imperial war-elephants driven into the crowd, crushing many protestors to death.</li>\n                <li><strong>Shivaji’s Famous Letter of Protest:</strong> Chhatrapati Shivaji addressed an eloquent, philosophical Persian letter to Aurangzeb, reminding him: <em>\"May it please your Majesty, that God is the God of all men, not the God of Muslims alone. Islam and Hinduism are only different terms used by Him. To display enmity towards any people's creed is to deface the masterpiece of the Divine Creator.\"</em></li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Demolition of Historic Temples</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The 1669 General Edict:</strong> In April 1669, Aurangzeb issued an imperial firman to all provincial governors ordering them <em>\"to demolish all schools and temples of the infidels and put down their teaching and religious practices.\"</em></li>\n                <li><strong>Destruction of Major Shrines:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Kashi Vishwanath Temple (Varanasi):</strong> Demolished in September 1669; on its foundation, the Gyanvapi Mosque was constructed using surviving temple walls and plinths.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Keshavdeva Temple (Mathura):</strong> Erected at a cost of 33 lakh rupees by Bir Singh Deo Bundela during Jahangir’s reign, this magnificent Krishna temple was razed in 1670, replaced by the Shahi Idgah Mosque, and the city was briefly renamed <em>Islamabad</em>.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Somnath Temple (Gujarat):</strong> Destroyed in 1665 and again in 1706.</li>\n                        <li>Dozens of temples in Mewar and Marwar were leveled following imperial military campaigns in 1679–1680.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. The Martyrdom of Guru Tegh Bahadur (November 1675 AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Kashmiri Pandits' Plea:</strong> In 1675, a delegation of Kashmiri Brahmins led by Pandit Kirpa Ram approached the ninth Sikh Guru, <strong>Guru Tegh Bahadur</strong>, at Anandpur Sahib, seeking protection against the forced conversions being orchestrated by Aurangzeb’s fanatical governor of Kashmir, Iftikhar Khan.</li>\n                <li><strong>Imperial Arrest and Torture:</strong> Guru Tegh Bahadur was arrested, brought to Delhi in an iron cage, and ordered by Aurangzeb to either embrace Islam or perform a miracle to prove his divinity. The Guru refused both demands, asserting the sacred inalienable right of every human being to freedom of conscience.</li>\n                <li><strong>Public Decapitation:</strong> After witnessing his loyal disciples (Bhai Mati Das sawn alive, Bhai Sati Das burned in cotton wool, and Bhai Dyala boiled alive in a cauldron) executed before his eyes, Guru Tegh Bahadur was publicly beheaded on <strong>November 11, 1675</strong> at Chandni Chowk, Delhi.</li>\n                <li><strong>Sacred Legacy:</strong> The site of his martyrdom is sanctified by the historic <strong>Gurdwara Sis Ganj Sahib</strong>, while the site where his severed body was cremated is marked by <strong>Gurdwara Rakab Ganj Sahib</strong>. His sacrifice earned him the revered title <strong>'Hind Di Chadar'</strong> (Shield of India).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. The Legal Masterpiece: Fatawa-i-Alamgiri</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>To establish uniform Islamic jurisprudence across his vast realm, Aurangzeb commissioned a monumental legal compendium named the <strong>Fatawa-i-Alamgiri</strong> (also known as <em>Al-Fatawa al-Hindiyya</em>).</li>\n                <li>Compiled over eight years (1667–1675) under the supervision of <strong>Shaikh Nizam of Lahore</strong> and an editorial board of roughly 500 eminent Islamic jurists at a cost of two lakh rupees, this six-volume Arabic compendium codified Hanafi Islamic law, regulating state administration, criminal justice, civil litigation, taxation, and family law.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
