            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Coronation of Shivaji at Raigad: Gaga Bhatt, Vedic Rites & Sovereign Coinage</h3>\n            <p>On June 6, 1674, Shivaji celebrated his formal Vedic coronation (<em>Rajyabhisheka</em>) at the mountain citadel of <strong>Raigad</strong>, transforming his de facto regional military power into a recognized, legitimate, and sovereign imperial monarchy.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Legal and Ideological Imperative</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Challenge of Legitimacy:</strong> Despite ruling over an extensive domain and commanding 100,000 troops, orthodox Brahmins and rival Maratha noble families (such as the Mores, Ghorpades, and Nimbalkars) questioned Shivaji’s legal right to sovereign rule, regarding him as a subordinate feudal jagirdar of the Bijapur Sultanate and technically of <em>Shudra</em> varna.</li>\n                <li><strong>Pandit Gaga Bhatt's Intercession:</strong> To resolve the constitutional dilemma, Shivaji invited <strong>Pandit Vishveshwar (Gaga Bhatt)</strong>, the foremost Vedic scholar and legal authority from Varanasi. After rigorous genealogical research, Gaga Bhatt established that the Bhonsle clan was directly descended from the solar dynasty of the <strong>Sisodia Rajputs of Mewar</strong> (tracing lineage back to Maharana Sajjan Singh, son of Rana Ajay Singh). Gaga Bhatt affirmed Shivaji's true <em>Kshatriya</em> status.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Grand Coronation Rituals (June 6, 1674 AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Purificatory Rites:</strong> Shivaji underwent the Vedic sacred thread ceremony (<em>Upanayana</em> / <em>Munj</em>), followed by ceremonial re-marriage to his chief queens according to Vedic Kshatriya rites.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Tuladan:</strong> He was weighed (<em>Tulapurusha Dan</em>) against gold, silver, pearls, and precious spices (weighing roughly 140 pounds / 16,000 gold huns), which were disbursed in charity to ascetics, scholars, and the poor.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Vedic Abhisheka:</strong> On the auspicious morning of the 13th day of the waxing moon of Jyeshtha (June 6, 1674), seated on a gold-plated throne cushioned with tiger skin, Shivaji was anointed with holy waters brought in golden pots from the seven sacred rivers of India (Ganga, Yamuna, Saraswati, Godavari, Narmada, Krishna, and Kaveri).</li>\n                <li>Gaga Bhatt held the jewel-studded golden umbrella over his head, loudly proclaiming him the sovereign monarch. English merchant Henry Oxenden, who attended the ceremony, witnessed the scene.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Imperial Titles, New Era & Independent Coinage</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Assumption of Sovereign Titles:</strong> Shivaji assumed the supreme titles:\n                    <ol>\n                        <li><strong>Chhatrapati:</strong> 'Lord of the Sovereign Umbrella' (Supreme Emperor).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Haidava Dharmoddharak:</strong> 'Redeemer and Protector of the Hindu Faith'.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Gau-Brahmana Pratipalak:</strong> 'Protector of Cows and the Pious'.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Kshatriya Kulavatamsa:</strong> 'Ornament of the Kshatriya Clan'.</li>\n                    </ol>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>The Rajyabhisheka Shaka:</strong> Shivaji founded a new sovereign calendar era, designated the <strong>Rajyabhisheka Shaka Era</strong>, dating state firmans from this landmark day.</li>\n                <li><strong>Independent Royal Coinage:</strong> As a declaration of complete monetary sovereignty, Shivaji struck independent coins in Devanagari script: the gold <strong>Hon</strong> (weighing roughly 2.8 grams) and the copper <strong>Shivrai</strong>, bearing the inscriptions <em>'Sri Raja Shiva'</em> on the obverse and <em>'Chhatrapati'</em> on the reverse.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Second Coronation (September 1674):</strong> Twelve days after the first coronation, Shivaji's mother Jijabai passed away. Viewing this and subsequent ill omens as astrological imbalances, Shivaji underwent a second, esoteric <strong>Tantric coronation</strong> on September 24, 1674, conducted by the renowned Tantric priest <strong>Nischal Puri Goswami</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
