            "id": "m20",
            "title": "Successors & The Peshwa Era: Sambhaji, Tarabai, Baji Rao I & The Maratha Confederacy (1680–1761 AD)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <h3>Successors & The Peshwa Era: Sambhaji, Tarabai, Baji Rao I & The Maratha Confederacy</h3>\n            <p>Following the sudden death of Chhatrapati Shivaji in April 1680, the Maratha nation weathered severe dynastic crises, emerged victorious from the 25-year war against Aurangzeb, and expanded under the charismatic leadership of the <strong>Bhat Peshwas</strong> into a pan-Indian empire.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Sambhaji, Rajaram & The Heroic Resistance of Maharani Tarabai</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Chhatrapati Sambhaji (1680–1689 AD):</strong> Reigned for nine heroic years, fighting simultaneously against the Mughals, the Portuguese, the Siddis of Janjira, and the British. He fought nearly 120 battles without suffering a defeat before his capture and martyrdom at Tulapur in March 1689.</li>\n                <li><strong>Chhatrapati Rajaram (1689–1700 AD):</strong> Shivaji’s younger son was crowned at Raigad. When Mughals besieged Raigad, Rajaram escaped to the distant Tamil fortress of <strong>Jinji</strong>, which became the epic center of Maratha resistance during an eight-year siege by Zulfiqar Khan (1690–1698). Maratha generals <strong>Santaji Ghorpade</strong> and <strong>Dhanaji Jadhav</strong> ravaged Mughal supply lines across the Deccan.</li>\n                <li><strong>Maharani Tarabai (1700–1707 AD):</strong> Following Rajaram’s death in 1700, his 25-year-old widow <strong>Maharani Tarabai</strong> (daughter of general Hambirrao Mohite) placed her infant son Shivaji II on the throne and assumed supreme command of the Maratha War of Independence.\n                    <ul>\n                        <li>Displaying astonishing military energy, she organized counter-offensives that crossed the Narmada River, carrying the war into the Mughal heartlands of <strong>Malwa and Gujarat</strong>.</li>\n                        <li>Historian Khafi Khan praised her: <em>\"She was a clever, intelligent woman, and had a great reputation for her civil and military management... Under her guidance, the Marathas showed a strength and resourcefulness never seen before.\"</em> It was Tarabai's unyielding resistance that broke Aurangzeb’s spirit and drove him to his death in 1707.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Rise of the Peshwas: Balaji Vishwanath (1713–1720 AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Upon Aurangzeb’s death, the Mughals released Sambhaji’s son, <strong>Chhatrapati Shahu</strong>, igniting a civil war between Shahu (at Satara) and Tarabai (at Kolhapur).</li>\n                <li>Shahu triumphed largely through the diplomatic genius of a Chitpavan Brahmin revenue officer named <strong>Balaji Vishwanath</strong>, whom Shahu appointed as <strong>Peshwa (Prime Minister)</strong> in November 1713, making the office hereditary.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Treaty of Delhi (1719 AD):</strong> Balaji Vishwanath marched to Delhi with 15,000 Marathas to aid the <em>Sayyid Brothers</em> in deposing Emperor Farrukhsiyar. In return, the Mughal Emperor granted the Marathas the formal imperial legal right (<em>Sanad</em>) to collect <strong>Chauth and Sardeshmukhi across the six Mughal Subahs of the Deccan</strong>, as well as recognizing Shahu's complete sovereignty over Swarajya. Historian Richard Temple called this treaty the <em>'Magna Carta of Maratha Dominion'</em>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Peshwa Baji Rao I (1720–1740 AD): Champion of Hindu-Pad-Padshahi</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Succeeding his father at age twenty, <strong>Baji Rao I</strong> was the greatest military cavalry general of Indian history, remaining <strong>undefeated across 41 pitched battles</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Strategic Vision:</strong> Baji Rao declared his grand imperial policy of <strong>Hindu-Pad-Padshahi</strong> (Pan-Hindu Empire): <em>\"Let us strike at the trunk of the withering tree [the Mughal Empire], and the branches will fall off themselves. Thus should the Maratha flag fly from the Krishna to the Indus!\"</em> Chhatrapati Shahu enthusiastically replied: <em>\"You shall fly it beyond the Himalayas! You are the worthy son of a worthy father!\"</em></li>\n                <li><strong>Masterpieces of Strategy:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Battle of Palkhed (1728 AD):</strong> Outmaneuvered Nizam-ul-Mulk of Hyderabad through brilliant cavalry mobility without firing a shot, forcing the Nizam to sign the Treaty of Shevgaon recognizing Shahu’s supremacy.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Raid on Delhi (March 1737 AD):</strong> Dashed across 500 miles in ten days with 500 horsemen, appearing outside the walls of Delhi and causing Emperor Muhammad Shah to panic, before retiring with imperial tribute.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Battle of Bhopal (1737 AD):</strong> Encircled and defeated the combined imperial armies of the Nizam and the Mughals, securing the cession of Malwa and the territory between the Narmada and Chambal.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Conquest of Salsette and Bassein (1739 AD):</strong> His brother Chimaji Appa defeated the Portuguese, liberating the northern Konkan.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>4. The Maratha Confederacy & Third Battle of Panipat (1761 AD)</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li>Under Baji Rao’s son, <strong>Peshwa Balaji Baji Rao (Nana Saheb, 1740–1761 AD)</strong>, the Maratha state expanded into a loose <strong>Maratha Confederacy</strong> governed by semi-autonomous dynastic chieftains:\n                    <ol>\n                        <li><strong>Peshwa:</strong> Governed Maharashtra from <strong>Pune</strong> (Shanivar Wada).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Scindia (Shinde):</strong> Master of <strong>Gwalior</strong> and northern India (Ranoji and later Mahadji Scindia).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Holkar:</strong> Governed <strong>Indore</strong> and Malwa (Malhar Rao Holkar).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Gaekwad:</strong> Controlled <strong>Baroda</strong> and Gujarat (Damaji Gaekwad).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Bhonsle:</strong> Governed <strong>Nagpur</strong>, Odisha, and raided Bengal (Raghuji Bhonsle).</li>\n                    </ol>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Panipat Climax (January 14, 1761 AD):</strong> The northward expansion of the Marathas (planting their flag at Attock in 1758 under Raghunath Rao) clashed with the Afghan invader <strong>Ahmad Shah Abdali</strong>. At the tragic <strong>Third Battle of Panipat</strong>, the Maratha army under Sadashivrao Bhau and Vishwasrao was destroyed, halting pan-Indian expansion and paving the way for the British East India Company.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
